## Installing the Chart

To install the chart, run the following:

```console
$ helm install kserve-llmisvc-resources oci://ghcr.io/kserve/charts/kserve-llmisvc-resources --version {{ template "chart.version" . }}
```

## Self-signed webhook certificates (without cert-manager)

By default the chart renders cert-manager `Issuer`/`Certificate` objects and annotates the webhook
configurations with `cert-manager.io/inject-ca-from`, so cert-manager must be installed. To install
without cert-manager, set `kserve.llmisvc.selfSignedCerts.enabled=true`. The chart then issues the
webhook serving certificate from the self-signed CA stored in the Secret named by
`kserve.llmisvc.selfSignedCerts.caSecretName` (created by the `kserve-llmisvc-crd` chart when its
matching flag is enabled), embeds the CA certificate directly as the webhook configurations'
`caBundle`, and renders no cert-manager objects. Certificates are reused from the cluster on upgrade
and never rotated.

Enable the flag on both charts together so the conversion webhook and the admission webhooks trust
the same CA, and install or upgrade `kserve-llmisvc-crd` first, in the same namespace. This chart
fails if the CA Secret is missing, because a CA of its own would not match the CRD conversion
webhook's.

### Switching an existing cert-manager install

On an install that already uses cert-manager, the `llmisvc-webhook-server-cert` Secret was issued by
cert-manager and is not owned by the Helm release, so the upgrade stops with an error until it is
removed. Delete the `Certificate` first (otherwise cert-manager re-creates the Secret), then the
Secret, then upgrade the `kserve-llmisvc-crd` chart followed by this chart with the flag enabled
(adjust `-n` to the release namespace):

```console
$ kubectl delete certificate llmisvc-serving-cert -n kserve --ignore-not-found
$ kubectl delete secret llmisvc-webhook-server-cert -n kserve
```

Expect brief admission and conversion webhook failures until both upgrades finish and the controller
serves the new certificate.

### GitOps and `helm template`

Self-signed mode relies on Helm's `lookup` to reuse the CA and serving certificate already on the
cluster, so it requires a real `helm install`/`helm upgrade` (including Flux's `HelmRelease`). It is
not supported with tools that render with `helm template` and no cluster access — e.g. Argo CD,
`helm template | kubectl apply`, or kustomize `helmCharts`. There, the two charts generate their CAs
independently, so the CRD conversion webhook never trusts the webhook serving certificate (even on a
one-off render), and continuously syncing tools also get new certificates on every render and never
converge. Use the default cert-manager mode with these tools.

